---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---
<Base
  title="ve — Examples"
  description="Move the clock through stored viscoelastic boundary element solutions: a megathrust, its afterslip, a subducting slab and a Burgers substrate, against a layered reference."
  current="examples"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>Examples<span class="sub">stored solves, moved through time</span></h1>
    <p>
      Nothing is solved here. Each model was solved in Python, once per
      Laplace state; this page adds those solutions together with the weights
      of one time, which is all a time is.
    </p>
    <div data-facts class="facts"></div>
    <div class="note">
      <b>Units.</b> Time is in Maxwell times of the substrate,
      &tau;&nbsp;=&nbsp;&eta;/&mu;: about ten years for
      10<sup>19</sup>&nbsp;Pa&nbsp;s and 30&nbsp;GPa. For the Burgers
      substrate &eta; is the steady viscosity, so its clock is the Maxwell
      models' own. Displacement is a
      fraction of the slip, and the velocity of a locked fault a fraction of
      the slip rate, measured from the fault slipping steadily.
    </div>
    <p class="contact">
      The surface is drawn through its elements' values, averaged to the
      nodes but never across the fault. It is the finest mesh as solved; the
      profile below it adds what the pair of meshes extrapolates to. The
      section is a plane of points at mid-strike, 3 or 4&nbsp;km apart, where
      the field was read from the boundary solution. Close to a boundary it
      is read from a continuous displacement fitted through the elements'
      values, because one value to an element would show through there. See
      <a href={`${base}concepts/`}>Concepts</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer" class="viewer">
    <div class="panel">
      <label><span>Model</span><span data-ctl="model"></span></label>
      <label><span>Show</span><span data-ctl="quantity"></span></label>
      <label><span>Colour by</span><span data-ctl="scalar"></span></label>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="stage"></div>
    <div data-timeline></div>
    <div data-bar class="bar"></div>
    <div data-slipbar class="bar" hidden></div>
    <div class="panel">
      <span data-sheets class="sheets"></span>
      <label><input data-elements type="checkbox" /><span>elements</span></label>
      <label><input data-cut type="checkbox" /><span>cut away the near half</span></label>
    </div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading&hellip;</p>

    <h2>Across the trench, at mid-strike</h2>
    <div data-timeline></div>
    <div data-profile class="profile"></div>
  </div>

  <p class="cap">
    <b>The slider is time.</b> Drag it, or press play, to move every sheet
    and the profile from the earthquake to 100 Maxwell times; the two sliders
    are one clock. It is logarithmic in time, so each decade has the same
    length, and its first stop is the earthquake itself. Between that stop
    and the first time the solves were laid out for (0.1 Maxwell times, or
    0.02 for the Burgers model) nothing was fitted, and the picture there is
    only a blend of the two. Pink outlines the
    fault. Every sheet is coloured by the quantity chosen and drawn where it
    is, to scale: nothing is exaggerated, and the motion is in the colour
    alone. The colour scale is fixed across time, so a pale early frame is a
    small early motion.
    <b>Deviatoric stress</b> is the size of the deviatoric part of the
    elastic stress, on a log scale over three decades; it lives in the
    volume, so only the section carries it and the other sheets are drawn
    plain. The profile is the surface within a narrow strip at mid-strike,
    against the same rupture in a plate over a half-space solved by a
    wavenumber integral that shares nothing with the boundary elements. See
    <a href={`${base}concepts/`}>Concepts</a> for what each curve is.
  </p>

  <script>
    import { startViewer } from "../viz/controls";
    startViewer(document.getElementById("viewer")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</Base>
